{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially after day ~7. Apply a downward level shift (larger for longer horizons).",
    "Trend/lag error in down-moves: when the recent 30d history is in a clear decline, the model mean-reverts upward too early and misses continued downside. Add a momentum term so forecasts keep falling for ~1–3 weeks after a strong downtrend.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: model projects sharp rebounds/spikes (local peaks) that do not materialize; realized path is smoother and lower. Damp forecast volatility by shrinking deviations from the recent mean (or from a fitted trend) by ~30–50%.",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–5) errors are small; mid/long horizon (days 10–30) overestimation grows materially. Use a bias that increases with horizon (e.g., subtract an additional amount each week ahead).",
    "Tail-risk underestimation of downside: the model rarely forecasts the deeper drawdowns seen in outcomes (e.g., low 60s). When recent volatility is high and trend is negative, widen downside adjustment (asymmetric correction: larger downward than upward)."
  ]
}
